 6. 🎒 Record low test scores 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: OECD . Chart: Erin Davis/Axios Test scores of 15-year-old students fell sharply worldwide in math, reading and science, according to new results from the influential PISA exam .

 Reading took the biggest hit, falling to the lowest level ever recorded in the assessment. "Hasty reading" — skimming fast but getting it wrong — nearly doubled between 2018 and 2025. OECD , which administers the test, found the sharpest declines in "the very skills that matter most in the AI age: evaluating information, making connections across multiple sources, and thinking critically about what we read." Math and science scores also hit record lows across 23 OECD countries. 🧪 Between the lines: U.S. science scores were a rare exception, ticking up three points from three years ago to match where they stood in 2009 and 2018.

 American reading and math scores both fell to their lowest on record. Read the full report . 

 
 
