 8. 🖼️ 1 fun thing: Tapestry mania 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 The 230-foot-long Bayeux Tapestry during a press preview at the British Museum in London. Photo: Adrian Dennis/AFP via Getty Images A blockbuster new exhibition at the British Museum in London showing the Bayeux Tapestry — an embroidered account of the Norman ​invasion of England in 1066 — has set off a national frenzy .

 Newspapers are running special ​guides, ticket strategies are circulating online and the pub opposite the museum temporarily changed its name to "The ​Beer Tapestry." The tapestry was displayed this week during a press preview at the British Museum in London. Photo: Kin Cheung/AP The masterpiece will be shown at the museum in central London for the next 10 months, after leaving France for the first time since the 11th century.

 Believed to have been sewn by English seamstresses before being taken to France, the embroidery tells the story of the events leading up to the Battle of Hastings — one of the most pivotal moments in European history. Explore the tapestry (NYT gift link). 

 
 
